        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Coherent Scattering",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "At very low energy (< 10 keV), an incident photon interacts with an atom's entire electron cloud, causing momentary vibration (excitation) \u2014 "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "no electrons are ejected and no ionization occurs",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ". The photon is re-emitted with the same energy but a new direction. Atomic number has no effect on this interaction. It's also called Rayleigh, Thompson, unmodified, or classical scatter, and its main diagnostic effect is blurring shadows on an image."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page4_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Coherent scattering results in:"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Ionization of the atom",
              "No ionization \u2014 the photon just changes direction"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Coherent scattering causes excitation, not ionization \u2014 no electron is ejected."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The Photoelectric Effect",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "An incoming photon is completely absorbed by a tightly-bound inner-shell electron, which is ejected as a "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "photoelectron",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ", creating an ion pair. It's most likely when the photon has just slightly more energy than the electron's binding energy."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "The photoelectric effect is "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "very dependent on atomic number (Z)",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " \u2014 probability increases sharply as Z increases. This is why bone (higher effective Z) produces strong shadows on diagnostic images and is responsible for image contrast; contrast increases as beam energy decreases. Photoelectric absorption is the "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "most common interaction in diagnostic imaging",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Compton Scattering",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A photon interacts with a loosely-bound outer-shell electron, ejecting it as a Compton (recoil) electron while the photon itself scatters in a new direction with reduced energy."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Compton scattering is "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "independent of atomic number",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " \u2014 it depends on electron density instead. It's the "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "most common interaction in radiation therapy",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " (megavoltage range) and is responsible for radiation fog on diagnostic images and for most shielding requirements, since scattered photons travel in unpredictable directions."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page6_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Which interaction dominates at megavoltage treatment energies?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Photoelectric effect",
              "Compton scattering"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Compton scattering is the dominant interaction at the megavoltage energies used in external beam radiation therapy."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Pair Production and Photodisintegration",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Pair production",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (threshold \u2265 1.02 MeV) occurs when a photon interacts with the nucleus's electric field and is absorbed, producing a negatron and a positron. The positron quickly annihilates, producing two 0.511 MeV photons traveling in opposite directions. It has no diagnostic imaging importance \u2014 it only matters at therapeutic energies."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Photodisintegration",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (\u2265 10 MeV) occurs when a photon is absorbed directly by the nucleus, which then emits a proton or neutron (a nucleon)."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "How Interactions Affect Image Quality",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "As beam energy (kVp) increases: photoelectric absorption decreases, Compton scattering increases, more photons transmit through without interacting, and image contrast decreases (since less differential absorption occurs)."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "A "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "decrease",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " in kVp produces "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "higher",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " contrast (more photoelectric absorption) but also "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "increased dose",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " to the patient \u2014 a classic imaging trade-off between contrast and dose."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Ready for the Simulator",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You've covered attenuation coefficients and HVL, and all five photon interactions: coherent scattering, the photoelectric effect, Compton scattering, pair production, and photodisintegration."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The Photon Interactions Simulator lets you select any of these five and watch an animated walkthrough of exactly what happens at the atomic level. Click below to explore it."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
